  [PASS] 103.2a the RANDOM budget is distance-led.  Because a_0 ~ D^-2.2 and half of SPARC has 20-30% Hubble-flow distances, distance is the largest per-galaxy channel in all three samples -- decisively so once the M/L is cut (38% of the variance), and only just so on the full deep tail, where inclination is its equal.  The stellar M/L's galaxy-to-galaxy scatter is a minor random term everywhere; its damage is done through the COHERENT zero-point   (full deep tail: distance 0.0195 dex vs Upsilon scatter 0.0096, inclination 0.0189, gas 0.0032; M/L-free sample: distance 0.0456 vs gas 0.0156)
  [PASS] 103.2b the COHERENT part is what the M/L cut actually buys.  On the full deep tail the Upsilon zero-point alone puts an irreducible 0.07 dex (17%) floor under any a_0 measured this way, no matter how many galaxies.  On the M/L-free sample that same channel drops by roughly a factor 5 and the coherent floor becomes the HI mass scale and the distance scale   (Upsilon zero-point: full deep tail 0.0522 dex, f_gas>0.7 0.0423, M/L-free 0.0121; coherent totals 0.0665 / 0.0607 / 0.0450 dex)

======================================================================================================================
3. THE FLOOR ONCE UPSILON IS REMOVED
======================================================================================================================
  all deep tail     : coherent floor WITH Upsilon = 0.0665 dex (17%), WITHOUT it = 0.0412 dex (10.0%)
  f_gas > 0.7       : coherent floor WITH Upsilon = 0.0607 dex (15%), WITHOUT it = 0.0436 dex (10.6%)
  f_*,loc < 0.2     : coherent floor WITH Upsilon = 0.0450 dex (11%), WITHOUT it = 0.0433 dex (10.5%)
  
  So the answer to the item's question -- what is the floor when Upsilon is removed -- is:
     10.5% in a_0, set by the distance SCALE (a_0 ~ D^-2.2, so the 8.3% H0 spread carried by the Hubble-flow galaxies is 19% in a_0 on its own) and by the HI mass scale.
     Neither of those is a galaxy-by-galaxy error and neither shrinks with sample size.
  [PASS] 103.3 with the stellar M/L removed the coherent floor falls from 17% to about 10%, and it is then DOMINATED BY THE DISTANCE SCALE, not by anything to do with baryons.  This is the sharpest statement in the item: the deep-tail a_0 is a distance-squared measurement, so it inherits twice the distance ladder's own fractional error   (floor without Upsilon = 0.0433 dex = 10.5%; distance-scale terms 0.0127 (TRGB) + 0.0343 (H0) vs gas zero-point 0.0232)

======================================================================================================================
4. THE CUT THAT WOULD REACH 3% -- and why it cannot be reached with SPARC
======================================================================================================================
  3% in a_0 = 0.0128 dex.  Three things must all be true at once:
    (i)   the COHERENT floor must be under 0.0128 dex.  For a sample of Hubble-flow galaxies the H0 term alone is |d log a_0/d log D| x 0.033 = 0.0744 dex, 5.8x the whole budget;
          for a purely TRGB/Cepheid sample it is 0.0225 dex, 1.8x the budget.
          => the distance ladder's zero-point would have to be known to 1.3% in D, i.e. better
             than the current TRGB/Cepheid calibration (~2%) and better than the Hubble tension's own spread.
    (ii)  the stellar M/L must be gone: at f_*,loc < 0.2 the Upsilon zero-point costs 0.0121 dex; on the full deep tail it costs 0.0522 dex, 4.1x the budget on its own.
    (iii) the RANDOM part must be beaten down: on the M/L-free sample it is 0.0590 dex with 23 galaxies, so it scales as 0.283/sqrt(N_gal).
          => 486 galaxies of the SAME quality, or fewer with better distances.
          with distance errors under 12% only (8 galaxies), the random term per galaxy is 0.143/sqrt(N) instead of 0.283/sqrt(N), so 124 such galaxies would do it -- SPARC has 8, a factor 16 short.

  THE NAMED CUT, stated as the item asks for it:
    'f_*,loc < 0.2 AND a TRGB or Cepheid distance' is the right cut -- it removes the M/L channel and the dominant
    random channel at once.  It needs about 124 galaxies where SPARC provides 8, so the
    measurement is data-limited by a factor of order 10 in sample size, and even with that sample it would stop at
    the 10.5% coherent floor unless the distance ZERO-POINT is improved to 1.3%.
    3% is therefore NOT reachable from rotation curves at all with today's distance ladder.  What is reachable is
    about 11%, and the way to get there is more TRGB distances to gas-dominated dwarfs.
  [PASS] 103.4 AGAINST THE ITEM'S OWN TARGET: 3% cannot be reached by this estimator on any cut of any rotation-curve sample, because a_0 ~ D^-2.2 turns the distance ladder's ~5% zero-point into a ~11% floor in a_0.  The item asked for the cut that reaches 3%; the honest answer is that no cut does, and the binding constraint is not baryonic physics but the distance scale   (distance-scale term alone = 0.0225 dex (TRGB) / 0.0744 dex (H0) vs the 3% target 0.0128 dex; reaching 3% needs the distance zero-point at 1.3%)

